<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Saint-Domingue</h1><p class="st-meta">1665 CE – 1804 CE · capital Cap-Français; later Port-au-Prince</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's richest colony, the world's leading sugar and coffee producer, whose enslaved population rose in 1791 and created independent Haiti.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>French buccaneers and planters spread from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tortuga">Tortuga</a> onto the western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> in the mid-17th century. In 1665 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-west-india-company">French West India Company</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-dogeron">Bertrand d'Ogeron</a> governor, who organised settlement, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> recognised French possession of the west of the island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ryswick">Treaty of Ryswick</a> in 1697.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>By the 1780s Saint-Domingue produced around 40 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and over half its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>. About half a million enslaved Africans, worked under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-noir">Code Noir</a> with appalling mortality, vastly outnumbered some 30,000 whites and a similar number of free people of colour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-haitien">Cap-Français</a> was the cultural capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a> the seat of government from 1770. Racial hierarchy and planter resentment of metropolitan trade rules ran deep.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> set off struggles among planters, free people of colour and, from August 1791, a vast slave uprising. France abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1793 to 1794. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a> rose to govern the colony, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> attempt to restore control failed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a> proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti's</a> independence on 1 January 1804.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/saint-domingue.jpg" alt="Saint-Domingue" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Bluebear2 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pavillon_LouisXIV.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1665 CE – 1804 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cap-Français; later Port-au-Prince</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bertrand d'Ogeron, Toussaint Louverture</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism and Vodou</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French and Haitian Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>French Saint-Domingue, Colony of Saint-Domingue</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1734&amp;focus=saint-domingue" aria-label="Saint-Domingue on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2724 1470 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Saint-Domingue lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3044 1726L3037 1692M3044 1726L5054 728M3044 1726L3044 1726M3044 1726L3041 1746"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3037 1692h0M5054 728h0M3044 1726h0M3041 1746h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3044 1726h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3044 1726h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1734&amp;focus=saint-domingue">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
